# RFP / Presales Pursuit Context

You are working in a **presales pursuit**. The EA Tooling Kit's full governance model applies here, but adapted for a time-boxed, commercially-driven context. The goal is not an architecture that will be governed for years — it is a credible, traceable, winning proposal that can be delivered.

This rule extends `togaf-core.mdc`. All TOGAF vocabulary and behavioural rules remain in force. Additional rules specific to presales work are below.

Read `ea-practice/rfp-pursuit-guide.md` for the Architecture Intent document format, C4 conventions, and the standard pursuit folder structure.

---

## RFP phase → TOGAF ADM mapping

Presales phases are not the ADM, but they draw from it. Understanding the mapping prevents the most common failure — treating architecture as a Phase 2 activity rather than a continuous thread from Phase 0.

| RFP Phase | ADM Equivalent | Key output |
|-----------|---------------|------------|
| Phase 0 — Qualify | Pre-ADM (commercial gate) | Bid/No-Bid Decision Record; go/no-go before architecture work begins |
| Phase 1 — Discovery | Phase A — Architecture Vision | Client Context Brief; NFR Catalogue; Solution Hypothesis; Architecture Intent |
| Phase 2 — Solution Design | Phases B/C/D | Capability Map; SAD; ADRs; Fit/Gap Assessment; NFR Response Matrix |
| Phase 3 — Delivery Model | Phases E/F (abbreviated) | Phased Roadmap; Risk Register; Assumptions & Exclusions |
| Phase 4 — Commercial | Outside ADM | Executive Summary; Compliance Matrix; Proposal assembly |
| Phase 5 — Presentation | Outside ADM | Orals deck; Clarification responses |

The most important transition is **Phase 1 → Phase 2**: the Architecture Intent document must be written and agreed before solution design begins. Everything in Phases 2–5 derives from it.

---

## The Architecture Intent — the single source of truth

The Architecture Intent is a one-to-two page document produced at the Phase 1/2 boundary. It is not a full SAD — it is the governing narrative that all workstreams read before producing their artifacts.

**It must exist before:**
- Options Analysis is written
- Effort estimation begins
- Executive Summary is drafted

**It contains:**
1. Problem statement — what the client is actually trying to solve (one paragraph)
2. Proposed capability approach — what capabilities the solution delivers, at ABB level
3. Key architectural decisions already made — with brief rationale (not full ADRs yet)
4. Assumed principles — which of the client's or engagement's principles govern this solution
5. Integration stance — how the solution connects to the existing estate (approach, not detail)
6. Explicit out-of-scope — what this solution does not address

Without this document, the failure pattern described in `ea-practice/rfp-pursuit-guide.md` is almost inevitable: executive summary says one thing, architecture diagrams say another, estimation assumes a third.

