## Vocabulary additions for RFP context

| Term | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| **Architecture Intent** | The one-to-two page governing narrative; single source of truth for the pursuit |
| **Pursuit** | A single RFP opportunity being responded to |
| **Pursuit lead** | The Principal or Director accountable for quality and coherence across all workstreams |
| **SAD** | Solution Architecture Document — the full reference architecture for the proposed solution |
| **NFR** | Non-Functional Requirement — performance, availability, security, compliance, scalability, etc. |
| **NFR Response Matrix** | How the proposed architecture addresses each NFR — traceability artifact |
| **Fit/Gap Assessment** | Requirement-level mapping: Met / Partial / Gap / Out-of-scope |
| **Compliance Matrix** | Maps every RFP requirement to the section of the proposal that responds to it |
| **Bid/No-Bid Decision Record** | Go/no-go decision with scoring against qualification criteria |
| **Open Questions Log** | Ambiguities that block architecture decisions; tracked until client responds |
| **Clarification Q&A Log** | Submitted clarification questions and client responses (living document) |
| **Assumptions & Exclusions** | Explicit scope boundary — what is assumed true and what is out of scope |
| **C4 model** | Context (C1) / Container (C2) / Component (C3) — preferred diagramming convention |

