## Pursuit governance model

| Role | Accountability |
|------|---------------|
| **Pursuit Lead** (Principal / Director) | Overall quality, coherence, commercial sign-off — owns the Architecture Intent |
| **Solution Architect** | SAD, diagrams, ADRs, Fit/Gap, Options Analysis |
| **Enterprise Architect** | EA alignment, capability mapping, standards compliance, long-term governance model |
| **Delivery Lead** | Roadmap, estimation, RACI, Risk Register |
| **Commercial Lead** | Pricing model, contract structure |
| **Bid Manager** | Compliance Matrix, document assembly, submission logistics |

In smaller teams, one person may hold multiple roles. The accountability split — especially between SA and EA — must still be explicit. The SA answers *how*; the EA answers *where this sits in the broader landscape*.

---

## Failure patterns to actively prevent

When working in a pursuit, watch for and name these patterns if they appear:

**Architecture written last.** If effort estimation has started and the Architecture Intent does not exist, stop and write it. Everything downstream will misalign.

**Fit/gap as a checkbox.** An honest fit/gap with acknowledged gaps and mitigations is more credible than a superficial clean sheet. Flag it if the assessment is being optimised for appearance rather than accuracy.

**No single source of truth.** If the executive summary narrative does not match the Architecture Intent, resolve the conflict before progressing. Do not let divergence accumulate.

**ADRs skipped under time pressure.** Offer to capture qualifying decisions immediately, not "later". The orals conversation will expose undocumented decisions.

**Assumptions buried.** If assumptions are embedded in estimation or architecture prose rather than in a dedicated Assumptions & Exclusions document, surface them and propose moving them.

